    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015110247": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Global Alliance for Laudato si'",
      "description": "This Postdoctoral Fellow position is being managed through a separate application system. We are sharing here to increase visibility and accessibility.  Apply  here  through September 18, 2026 (DEADLINE EXTENDED) if you are interested. The University of Notre Dame invites applications for Postdoctoral Research Fellows to advance the research agenda of the Global Alliance for Laudato si'. The fellows will pursue an independent line of scholarship in integral ecology while supporting the collaborative research of the Alliance's five thematic working groups. The Global Alliance for Laudato si' is an international network of Catholic and mission-aligned researchers, educators, and practitioners, launched in March 2026 through a partnership between Notre Dame's Just Transformations to Sustainability Initiative and the Laudato Si' Center for Higher Education. Its inaugural convening at the Borgo Laudato Si' drew roughly 100 participants from five continents. Laudato si'-inspired scholarship has grown substantially over the past decade but remains fragmented, under-resourced, and disconnected across regions and disciplines. The Alliance exists to build the relational, intellectual, and digital infrastructure that allows this work to cohere. Five working groups formed at the inaugural convening and now carry the Alliance's research program: Place-Based Collective Action for Integral Ecology Translating Knowledge to Action at the Food-Water-Energy Nexus Transforming Economic Systems Towards Sustainability and Justice Reimagining Education for Integral Ecology Theological Roots of Integral Ecology The fellows will distribute work across the five working groups according to background and expertise, helping members move from shared interest to publishable, actionable scholarship. Because the working groups span environmental science, political science, theology, economics, and education, this position suits interdisciplinary scholars who are able to collaborate across fields and geographies. The postdoctoral fellow will assume the following key responsibilities: Design and carry out an independent research project in integral ecology that contributes to the agenda of one of the Alliance's working groups. Support the working groups in sharpening research questions, identifying collaborators, and developing shared outputs, in coordination with the Alliance's Program Manager. Synthesize scholarship across the five groups and identify where their questions intersect, so that the Alliance's research agenda develops as a whole rather than as five separate tracks. Co-author and support interdisciplinary publications with Alliance members, including scholars for whom English is a second or language. Contribute to the Alliance's knowledge infrastructure, including the searchable member database and shared research resources. Support convenings at the Borgo Laudato Si' and elsewhere, including preparation of background materials and follow-up documentation. Engage in collaborative grant writing and proposal development to sustain and expand the Alliance's work. The fellows will  join a cohort of postdoctoral scholars supported by the Just Transformations to Sustainability Initiative, Notre Dame's University-wide effort to build a sustainable future where people and nature flourish together. The cohort works on sustainability research that engages ecological, social, economic, and ethical dimensions together rather than in isolation. This is a full-time, in-person position with an initial appointment of one year, renewable for an additional year on the basis of satisfactory performance and availability of funding. \n\n Ph.D. (in hand by the starting date) in a field relevant to integral ecology, such as environmental science, political science, philosophy, anthropology, sociology, economics, education, and public health. Applicants with interdisciplinary degrees are welcome. Demonstrated research expertise in at least one of the five working group areas, and a clear account of how that expertise would contribute to the others. A publication record appropriate to career stage in a field directly relevant to the Alliance's work. Experience collaborating across disciplinary and institutional boundaries, and with researchers and practitioners outside North America and Europe. Excellent communication skills, including the ability to write for academic and practitioner audiences. Willingness to travel internationally, including to Rome. \n\n The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The  University of Notre Dame  is more than a workplace! We are a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015109307": {
      "title": "Part-Time Culinary Service Associate | South and North Dining Halls",
      "description": "The University of Notre Dame Operations, Events, and Safety  (UOES) division is currently hiring for part-time Culinary Service Associates in  South and North Dining Halls  in our Campus Dining department. The UOES division oversees the University's event management, hospitality, and customer service functions, while also supporting student life, academic initiatives, research activities, and annual milestone celebrations.   Culinary Service Associate The  Culinary Service Associate (CSA)  is a “back-of-house” position. This position is critical to ensuring all diners experience excellent food production and service. This person is responsible for cleaning kitchen equipment, removing trash, cleaning floors and ceiling tiles, assisting with set-up and operation of the dish room, and participating in other general assignments.   Key Responsibilities: Maintain the cleanliness and visual appeal of serving lines, food displays, and condiment stations throughout service hours Assist with basic food preparation tasks such as washing and cutting vegetables, portioning ingredients, preparing simple salads, or assembling sandwiches/wraps as directed by culinary staff Monitor and replenish food items on serving lines, ensuring a continuous supply and alerting cooks to low stock Operate and maintain basic kitchen equipment safely, such as toasters, warmers, beverage dispensers, and coffee machines Clean and sanitize workstations, serving utensils, and equipment before, during, and after shifts Assist with dishwashing duties, including scraping, loading, and unloading the dish machine, and proper storage of clean dishware Support inventory management by assisting with the receiving, rotating, and proper storage of food and supplies \n\n Capable of managing time effectively with minimal supervision Positive, respectful, and committed to the schedule you create Excellent communication and listening skills Ability to maintain a standing position for extended periods of time (up to 6 hours at a time). Ability to move around the café to attend to the needs of guests \n\n Pay Rate: $17.50/hr The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Hospitality/Dining/Catering"
    },
