    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015108381": {
      "title": "Temporary | Baker (Center for Culinary Excellence)",
      "description": "The University of Notre Dame Operations, Events, and Safety  (UOES) division is currently hiring for temporary/on-call Bakers within our campus Hospitality department. The UOES division oversees the University's event management, hospitality, and customer service functions, while also supporting student life, academic initiatives, research activities, and annual milestone celebrations. Baker This position works under the guidance of senior bakers and culinary leadership, performing a range of tasks from basic prep to complex pastry production. Ideal for candidates with varied levels of experience, this role requires adaptability, attention to detail, and a commitment to food quality, safety, and hospitality. Key Responsibilities: Prepare and bake a variety of bakery items, from basic pastries (e.g., cookies, muffins, cakes) to more complex products such as laminated doughs, mousse cakes, artisan breads, and plated desserts. Measure, scale, mix, and assemble ingredients accurately according to standardized recipes and chef instructions. Operate and maintain kitchen equipment such as mixers, ovens, and proofers safely and efficiently. Participate in the decoration and finishing of pastries, including glazing, piping, and plating. Maintain mise en place and support event production, including live event stations and customer-facing service, when needed. Follow and uphold food safety and sanitation protocols (ServSafe standards). Maintain a clean, organized, and safe work environment; ensure proper storage of ingredients and finished goods. Collaborate with culinary team members to meet production schedules, quality standards, and service needs for daily operations and special events. \n\n Prior baking/pastry experience preferred; open to entry-level candidates with strong interest and willingness to learn. Ability to work independently and as part of a team in a fast-paced environment. Strong organizational and time-management skills. Knowledge of or ability to learn kitchen safety, hygiene, and food handling standards. Flexible availability, including early mornings, evenings, weekends, and event-based shifts.   \n\n Pay Rate: $17.50/hr The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015108676": {
      "title": "Temporary | Dish Crew Steward (The Morris Inn)",
      "description": "The University of Notre Dame Operations, Events, and Safety  (UOES) division is currently hiring for temporary Dish Crew Stewards within  The Morris Inn  within our campus Hospitality department. The UOES Division oversees the University's event management, hospitality, and customer service functions, while also supporting student life, academic initiatives, research activities, and annual milestone celebrations.   Dish Crew Steward This entry-level position is perfect for individuals starting their journey in a professional kitchen environment. As a Dish Steward Crew Member, you'll develop foundational skills in kitchen sanitation, organization, and support, ensuring the smooth flow of operations. This position is intentionally structured with growth in mind. Individuals who demonstrate reliability, initiative, and strong communication skills will be well-positioned to take on increased responsibilities and potential leadership roles as the department evolves. Future opportunities may include deeper involvement in event logistics, equipment management, and front-of-house and back-of-house collaboration - skills essential for delivering elevated service standards and ensuring a seamless guest experience. Key Responsibilities: Operate and maintain the Champion Industries Model EUCCW8 dishwasher, ensuring proper washing, rinsing, and sanitizing procedures. Keep the dish area clean and organized throughout your shift, adhering strictly to all cleaning and sanitation guidelines for food safety and health code compliance. Pot & Pan Sink: Manually scrub and sanitize pots, pans, and kitchen tools. Dirty Table & Loader: Scrape, sort, and efficiently load dirty dishes into the machine. Clean Catch Side: Unload, inspect, and organize clean dishes for proper storage. Clean Dish Restocking: Transport and restock clean dishes, glassware, and utensils to designated areas. Utility Support (5th Man): Assist as needed, including emptying trash, cleaning tilts and kettles, spot sweeping/mopping, and restocking/sorting silverware. Secondary Responsibilities: Assisting culinary staff with light prep, plating, and food distribution for both plated and buffet-style service Pulling and organizing equipment for events (e.g., chafers, platters, plates, etc.) Sorting and staging serviceware for FOH event teams Transporting hot/cold boxes to and from event spaces Returning equipment to the dish room and ensuring it is cleaned and stored in its proper location       \n\n Ability to thrive in a fast-paced, team-oriented kitchen environment. Strong attention to detail with an unwavering commitment to cleanliness and organization. Physical capability to lift up to 50 lbs, stand for extended periods, and perform tasks such as bending to clean floor drains and climbing equipment/ladders. Highly dependable with a positive attitude and a proactive willingness to assist the team as needed.   \n\n Pay Rate: $17.50/hr The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015106209": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant | Landscape Services As the Administrative Assistant, you will provide comprehensive administrative and operational support to the Landscape Services team within Facilities Design and Operations. This role is responsible for a variety of office, recordkeeping, procurement, financial, and coordination activities that keep the department running smoothly. As the primary administrative contact for Landscape Services, you will work closely with departmental employees, contractors, vendors, and campus partners to keep information moving, priorities organized, and daily operations on track. Things move quickly in this role, and no two days are exactly the same. You’ll need to be highly organized, detail-oriented, and comfortable managing multiple priorities while working with a variety of people. If you’re someone who notices what needs to happen next, takes initiative, and makes sure it gets done, you’ll thrive in this role. What You’ll Do Administrative Operations Serve as the primary administrative contact for Landscape Services, responding to inquiries from departmental staff, campus partners, contractors, and vendors. Coordinate calendars, schedule meetings, reserve spaces, and assist with departmental events, training sessions, and employee communications. Prepare, compile, and distribute correspondence, reports, spreadsheets, presentations, and other departmental documents. Financial and Procurement Support Coordinate departmental purchasing activities, including creating requisitions, processing purchase orders, and monitoring procurement transactions. Process and track accounts payable invoices while ensuring compliance with University policies and procedures. Assist with budget monitoring, expense tracking, and financial recordkeeping. Maintain financial documentation to support audits, budget reviews, and reporting activities. Records and Information Management Maintain accurate departmental records, databases, and filing systems. Manage equipment inventories, maintenance records, key control documentation, vehicle records, and vendor information. Ensure records are maintained in accordance with University policies and records retention requirements. Safeguard confidential information and maintain data integrity across departmental systems. Operational Coordination Track and monitor maintenance requests, work orders, and service activities, communicating with appropriate personnel to ensure timely completion. Coordinate communication and workflow between Landscape Services staff, contractors, vendors, and campus stakeholders. Monitor office and operational supply inventories and coordinate ordering and distribution of materials as needed. Assist with documentation related to departmental safety, compliance, and operational activities. Employee and Departmental Support Support employee administrative processes, including onboarding, timekeeping documentation, training coordination, and employee communications. Assist with staff meetings, recognition activities, and department-wide initiatives. Provide responsive, customer-focused support to employees and campus constituents while fostering positive working relationships. Special Projects and Continuous Improvement Participate in departmental projects and initiatives that support operational efficiency and service excellence. Assist with process improvements, data collection, and reporting efforts to support departmental goals. Perform other duties and special assignments as needed to support the mission and objectives of Landscape Services and Facilities Design and Operations. Why Notre Dame At Notre Dame, you’ll find more than a job, you’ll find a career with purpose, a community that values what you bring, and an opportunity to make a meaningful impact every day! You’ll be part of a collaborative and supportive environment where your work helps create an exceptional experience for students, faculty, staff, and visitors while contributing to the mission of the University. \n\n Required Education High School Diploma or GED Required Experience Three to four years of administrative support, office management, or related experience. Required Skills Proficiency with Google Workspace (Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Docs, Sheets, and Meet) and Microsoft Office (Outlook, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint). Ability to learn and effectively use departmental and University systems, including procurement, financial, scheduling, and other administrative applications. Strong calendar management skills, including coordinating meetings, managing multiple calendars, resolving scheduling conflicts, and tracking meeting details. Excellent verbal, written, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with faculty, staff, students, vendors, and other campus partners. Strong organizational skills with the ability to prioritize competing demands, manage multiple tasks, and follow through in a fast-paced environment. High attention to detail and a commitment to accuracy when managing records, schedules, financial information, and administrative documentation. Ability to work independently, use sound judgment, anticipate needs, and take initiative while knowing when to seek guidance. Preferred Qualifications Experience supporting operations, facilities, maintenance, landscaping, construction, or similar environments. Experience with procurement, invoicing, accounts payable, or financial recordkeeping. \n\n Application Deadline to Apply : September 22, 2026 Required Documents to Apply : Resume & Cover Letter Hiring Pay Range: Up to $44,000 The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace! We are a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Office/Clerical"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015105806": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate",
      "description": "We are looking for a Senior Research Associate with deep expertise in character education, virtue development, and higher education to become a core member of the Virtues & Vocations team at the Institute for Social Concerns. Virtues & Vocations is a national social movement to reimagine the cultivation of character across the professions. This aspirational, cross-professional learning community understands thriving professions are the backbone of thriving societies and knows professional excellence requires both competence and character. Rooted in the University of Notre Dame’s founding commitment to vocational formation guided by a storied tradition of distinctively multi-disciplinary virtue education and scholarship, Virtues & Vocations invites students, scholars, and practitioners to experience the wonder and joy of a movement that fosters character in context and boldly contributes to the revival of genuine morality in civil society and the professions. Virtues & Vocations hosts faculty workshops, an annual conference, and monthly webinars, and engages issues of character, professional identity, and moral purpose through a bi-annual magazine, an annual peer reviewed academic journal, and a soon-to-be-launched web-based resource hub that includes explainer videos, short documentaries, case studies, syllabi, sample assignments, and articles. The Senior Research Associate will be a key contributor to a fast-paced, entrepreneurial team, contributing to a range of projects. Within the Virtues & Vocations portfolio, this role is particularly tasked with the development and implementation of the Integrating Virtue Together program, which includes designing, delivering, and evaluating a series of workshops for faculty from across the country who are committed to cultivating virtues through their courses. The ideal candidate will be an expert in this field and will provide high-touch support and guidance to faculty as they redesign their courses based on the program’s teachings. They will also support the IVT alumni network, developing communications, resources and programming that is responsive to our alumni network, which includes faculty from more than 80 colleges and universities. The Senior Research Associate will also translate the IVT framework for use on our web platform, will provide editorial support for Virtues & Vocations’ publications, and will write feature articles and case studies. We seek an energized colleague who is capable of managing multiple simultaneous projects and who can work independently and collaboratively to enhance the Institute’s programs through research and the identification of best practices. Strong experience in writing and virtue pedagogy is a must. This is a five-year, limited-term position. It is renewable contingent upon funding.  What You Will Do Lead the Integrating Virtue Together program through the design, delivery, and evaluation of our workshop series. Provide mentoring and support for faculty and alumni participating in the program. Connect current research to the programmatic development of virtue and justice and incorporate these connections into Virtues & Vocations programming. Facilitate connections with Notre Dame faculty who are interested in making virtue more central to their courses and programs. Identify and apply for grants to provide ongoing support for the program. Serve as an editor for our magazine, Virtues and Vocations: Higher Education for Human Flourishing, and our academic journal, The Annual Review of Virtue and the Professions. Write one to two articles per month for our newsletter. Write short case studies and sample assignments for our web-based platform. \n\n Doctoral degree with or Master’s degree with 5 years of relevant experience. Experience developing scholarly networks nationally and internationally. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Teaching and program development experience. Ability to organize and present research material to professional audiences. Must be able to work independently in a fast-paced environment, manage multiple projects, and prioritize activities in response to changing circumstances and ongoing deadlines. \n\n RESUME & COVER LETTER ARE REQUIRED to be considered for this position. Please add in the attachments section of your application.  Pay range: commensurate with experience Please submit your application by September 10, 2026.   This position is a fully on-site position in South Bend, Indiana Benefits page: https://hr.nd.edu/benefits-compensation/ The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame, founded in 1842 in Notre Dame, Indiana, is a premier American research university. As a Catholic institution, it uniquely blends faith with intellectual inquiry, striving for excellence in teaching, research, and serving the common good. Notre Dame offers a dynamic and rewarding environment for its diverse community of students, faculty, and staff, who are all dedicated to fostering an inclusive campus and being a force for good in the world. With world-class facilities, acclaimed academic programs, and a strong commitment to innovation and ethical leadership, Notre Dame is an ideal place to make a meaningful impact.",
      "department": "Administration/Leadership"
    },
