    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015042816": {
      "title": "Temporary | Dish Crew (LaFortune Student Center)",
      "description": "The  University of Notre Dame’s Operations, Events, and Safety (UOES) Division  is currently hiring for temporary/on-call Dish Crew Stewards. UOES leads the University’s event management, hospitality, and customer service operations, supporting student life, academic programs, research initiatives, athletics, alumni relations, and major campus events. Dish Crew  \n \nThis entry-level position is perfect for individuals starting their journey in a professional kitchen environment. As a Dish Crew member, you'll develop foundational skills in kitchen sanitation, organization, and support, ensuring the smooth flow of operations. This position is intentionally structured with growth in mind. Individuals who demonstrate reliability, initiative, and strong communication skills will be well-positioned to take on increased responsibilities and potential leadership roles as the department evolves. Future opportunities may include deeper involvement in event logistics, equipment management, and front-of-house and back-of-house collaboration - skills essential for delivering elevated service standards and ensuring a seamless guest experience. \n \n Key Responsibilities: Operate and maintain the dishwasher, ensuring proper washing, rinsing, and sanitizing procedures. Keep the dish area clean and organized throughout your shift, adhering strictly to all cleaning and sanitation guidelines for food safety and health code compliance. Pot & Pan Sink: Manually scrub and sanitize pots, pans, and kitchen tools. Dirty Table & Loader: Scrape, sort, and efficiently load dirty dishes into the machine. Clean Catch Side: Unload, inspect, and organize clean dishes for proper storage. Clean Dish Restocking: Transport and restock clean dishes, glassware, and utensils to designated areas. Utility Support (5th Man): Assist as needed, including emptying trash, cleaning tilts and kettles, spot sweeping/mopping, and restocking/sorting silverware.   \n\n Ability to thrive in a fast-paced, team-oriented kitchen environment. Strong attention to detail with an unwavering commitment to cleanliness and organization. Physical capability to lift up to 50 lbs, stand for extended periods, and perform tasks such as bending to clean floor drains and climbing equipment/ladders. Highly dependable with a positive attitude and a proactive willingness to assist the team as needed. \n\n Pay Rate:  $17.50/hr Schedule: Sunday/Monday - 1:30PM-10PM Friday/Saturday - 9AM-5PM The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015042566": {
      "title": "Director, Academic Advancement: Affordability & Access",
      "description": "The University of Notre Dame is a Catholic academic community of higher learning, and our ability to successfully fundraise for our academic ambitions will help define our future. Unique in higher education, this is a role that is simultaneously at the heart of our academic community, and at the heart of our fundraising efforts, with the ability to influence both. The Director, Academic Advancement for Affordability and Access is charged with proactively and effectively connecting the Enrollment Division with the University's Department of Development, with the objective of securing philanthropy for stated priorities within the Division. Effective individuals will take high-level academic concepts and complex initiatives and package them for fundraisers so that potential benefactors can see exactly how their gift changes the world. In particular, this role will be raising funding to support financial aid, Pathways to Notre Dame initiatives, and access programming (campus visit days and Pre-College programs). The individual will report to the Senior Director, Academic Advancement, and in pursuit of funding for the Enrollment Division, will serve four primary constituents:      1) The VP for Undergraduate Enrollment;      2) Development Fundraisers;      3) University donors;      4) Leadership and other staff of the Department of Development. This individual is expected to meet annual collaborative production goals as developed with Academic Advancement leadership. To meet this annual goal, the individual will:      1) Build and actively manage a proposal pipeline;       2) Develop formal proposals to be presented to donors; and       3) Advance proposals from inception to conclusion.       4) Lead and liaise on a number of other endeavors that span Development and the academic units, such as Advisory Councils, immersions and educational sessions, and donor prospecting, cultivation, and stewardship.   KEY ACTIVITIES & GOALS: In collaboration with the Vice President of the Enrollment Division, develop a vision for fundraising priorities that is understandable and promotable by fundraisers and resonates with an external audience.  In partnership with Development fundraisers, identify, cultivate, solicit, and steward fundraising prospects with capacity and inclination to make leadership level gifts.  Advance within the Enrollment Division a culture of philanthropy and awareness of Development’s expectations, policies, processes, and procedures through formal and informal engagement of the Dean, faculty, and staff. Creatively develop messaging, packaging, and engagement opportunities around major initiatives for external and internal audiences. Educate Development fundraisers on current priorities and needs pertaining to Affordability & Access.  Administer the Affordability & Access Advisory Council as a fundraising vehicle. \n\n Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s Degree. 5 – 6 years of professional or academic work experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Evidence of a proactive approach to advancing projects from inception to conclusion. Evidence of ability to influence, persuade, and earn the trust of senior leadership to work with you toward a shared goal. Record of success in translating complex concepts into clear, compelling narratives. Evidence of effective collaboration and ability to work in a fast-paced environment. Ability to manage multiple priorities and projects. Ability to think and plan strategically for both short and long term goals. Proven success accomplishing stated metrics and goals. Must be legally authorized to work in the United States. Certain qualities will be key to success in this role. They include:  Deep commitment to Notre Dame’s mission and core values. Ability to inspire and engender a passion for Notre Dame. Unparalleled personal integrity. Persistence, resilience, and grit. Strong initiative and an outcomes-oriented work style. Optimistic and empowered attitude. Creative and entrepreneurial mindset. Intellectual curiosity in a wide range of interests related to the dimensions of an academic institution like Notre Dame. Preferred Qualifications: Advanced degree. Sales and Marketing experience. Deep familiarity with national landscape of higher education financial aid, access, and affordability. 7+ years of experience in relationship management with a track record of closing six- and seven-figure gifts. \n\n RESUME AND COVER LETTER REQUIRED to be considered for this position.  Please add in the attachments section of the application.     Application Deadline to Apply : September 15, 2026 Salary : Starting in the low $110s, Commensurate with experience Benefits page:  https://hr.nd.edu/benefits-compensation/ The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity.",
      "department": "Development/Fundraising"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015035476": {
      "title": "F&B Manager, Rohr's",
      "description": "The Food & Beverage Manager of Rohr’s is a hands-on leadership role responsible for the daily operations of Rohr’s Tavern, Bistro, Terrace, and In-Room Dining at the Morris Inn. Reporting to the Assistant Director of Restaurants and Cafes, the Manager leads by example to deliver an unforgettable guest experience that consistently meets or exceeds AAA Four Diamond standards. As the “conductor” of the dining room floor, the Manager coordinates front-of-house and culinary teams to ensure seamless, timely, and elevated service. This position provides leadership throughout the full employee lifecycle, including training, coaching, performance management, and recognition. The Manager is also responsible for maximizing retail revenue opportunities, maintaining operational excellence, and demonstrating exceptional stewardship of University resources. Key Responsibilities Guest Experience & Service Excellence Lead daily dining operations across Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner, Late Night, and In-Room Dining. Deliver a consistently elevated guest experience, with thoughtful and seamless offerings for game days and key annual university event weekends Direct and coordinate the dining room floor, ensuring effective communication between hosts, servers, bartenders, culinary teams, and other operational partners. Monitor service pace, beverage quality, dining room atmosphere, and overall guest satisfaction. Address guest needs and service concerns promptly, professionally, and effectively. Maintain service standards that meet or exceed AAA Four Diamond expectations. Team Leadership & Employee Development Lead by example through a visible, hands-on management approach. Onboard, train, coach, and develop food and beverage team members. Foster an inclusive, high-energy hospitality culture built on teamwork, accountability, and recognition. Provide proactive coaching and performance feedback to support employee growth and operational consistency. Establish clear expectations and hold team members accountable for service and performance standards. Recognize and celebrate employee contributions and successes. Operational Excellence Oversee daily operations of restaurant and In-Room Dining. Develop and manage complex staffing schedules to align labor resources with business demands. Monitor labor costs and productivity while maintaining appropriate service levels. Manage inventory, ordering, product utilization, and operational supplies. Ensure compliance with established University, hospitality, safety, sanitation, and food and beverage standards. Identify operational opportunities and implement solutions that improve efficiency, service, and profitability. Revenue & Financial Stewardship Maximize retail revenue opportunities across Rohr’s and In-Room Dining. Monitor sales performance and identify opportunities to increase revenue and profitability. Manage labor, inventory, and other operating resources responsibly. Demonstrate exceptional stewardship of University resources while balancing financial performance with guest and employee experience. Support initiatives that drive sales, improve productivity, and enhance the overall financial performance of the outlets. Key Focus Areas Retail Excellence: Orchestrate daily dining periods with a strong focus on service pace, beverage quality, guest engagement, atmosphere, and revenue generation. Unified Hospitality Culture :  Cultivate an inclusive, high-energy team environment through hands-on leadership, coaching, collaboration, accountability, and proactive recognition. Operational Precisio n:  Effectively manage scheduling, staffing, inventory, labor costs, and service execution across multiple restaurant outlets and guest room service. \n\n Education Bachelor’s degree preferred in Hospitality Management, Restaurant Management, Business, or a related field required. Experience 5–6 years of progressive experience in food and beverage operations, including at least 5 years of supervisory or management experience. Demonstrated experience leading and developing a large or complex team of employees. Experience managing food and beverage outlets or high-volume hospitality operations preferred. Experience with creative and elevated food and beverage presentations is preferred. Skills & Competencies Strong organizational and time-management skills with the ability to effectively manage multiple priorities. Creative approach to food and beverage presentation, service, and guest experience. Demonstrated leadership skills with the ability to motivate, coach, and hold employees accountable. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to work collaboratively with culinary, service, and operational teams. Strong attention to detail and commitment to hospitality and service excellence. Ability to make sound decisions and effectively resolve operational and guest-service challenges. \n\n Annual Salary: Starting at $60,000, Commensurate with experience Application Deadline: September 18, 2026 Application Materials: Resume (Required) and Cover Letter (Preferred) The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Hospitality/Dining/Catering"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015030116": {
      "title": "Program Coordinator for Alcohol Education",
      "description": "The McDonald Center for Student Well-Being enhances student flourishing and embeds holistic well-being into every aspect of student life. Through health promotion, harm reduction, prevention education, and the cultivation of student leaders in well-being, the McDonald Center empowers all students to make healthy choices while inspiring a campus community to create a culture of well-being. Working within a public health framework, the Program Coordinator for Alcohol Education will work with a dynamic team of professionals to grow a strong peer-driven well-being culture as a means of embedding well-being into every aspect of student life. This position reports directly to the Director for McDonald Center for Student Well-Being and will  \nwork collaboratively with the Assistant Director, Substance Use Prevention and Education as part of Student Health and Wellness in the Division of Student Affairs. This is a full-time, 12 month position and will require weekend and evening hours.  Alcohol Education and Substance Misuse Prevention   Assist with the development and implementation of a cohesive campus-wide alcohol education campaign for all students (e.g., Stay in the Green). Design, implement, and assess alcohol education and substance use risk reduction strategies. Programming will include sober tailgates, substance-free late night events, and off-campus excursions. These responsibilities will primarily occur during non-business hours.   Co-develops psychoeducation curricula, presentations, and workshops,  grounded in prevention and promotion models tailored to different student experiences represented on campus. Student Initiatives Coordination Cultivate a substance-free and sober curious community by supporting ZeroProof, a student organization affiliated with the McDonald Center. Recruit, train, and mentor peer leaders, volunteers, and student employees.  Manage budget for peer-led initiatives and training. Other Opportunities  Provide individual and group alcohol education meetings for students. Serve on campus committees; attend and present at regional and national training and conferences. Regularly collaborates with other areas within the Division of Student Affairs and Student Health and Wellness, student clubs and organizations, and other University units to enhance the department’s work building connections and relationships with students. \n\n Bachelor’s degree in Health Promotion, Health Science, Public Health, Psychology, or other related field. Skills Demonstrates strong leadership and interpersonal skills for empowering, motivating, and developing students. Demonstrate high standards of integrity, and motivation to incorporate best practices into organizational culture. Strong communication skills (written and verbal) that contribute to building and sustaining effective collaboration with University and community partners. Ability to deliver compelling and effective presentations and educational experiences for a variety of audiences. Demonstrates strong organizational skills and ability to manage multiple projects and priorities. \nStrong computer skills: Microsoft Office suite, Google applications, Social media platforms, graphic design The successful candidate will also demonstrate a clear appreciation of and commitment to the University’s Catholic, Holy Cross mission.  \n\n Salary:$47,500 Applications will be accepted through September 23, 2026 RESUME & COVER LETTER ARE REQUIRED to be considered for this position. Please add in the attachments section of your application. The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace! We are a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Student Affairs"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015022586": {
      "title": "Mechanic B",
      "description": "The Mechanic B is an entry-level position responsible for assisting with the maintenance, repair, and overhaul of Power Plant equipment and related auxiliary systems. This position supports the reliable, safe, and economical operation of utility services for the University. The Mechanic B works through a prescribed program of training, coursework, and hands-on practical experience designed to develop the knowledge and skills necessary to independently perform the duties of a Mechanic B. Equipment supported by this position includes steam turbine generators, steam boilers, heat recovery steam generators, chillers, diesel generators, air compressors, pumps, fans, and other power plant and utility equipment. Key Responsibilities Assist with the maintenance, inspection, repair, and overhaul of Power Plant equipment and systems. Assist in the maintenance and repair of steam turbine generators, 400 psi steam boilers, heat recovery steam generators, and associated equipment. Support maintenance activities for steam turbine and electric-driven chillers used for air conditioning. Assist with the maintenance and repair of diesel generators, low-pressure air compressors, pumps, fans, and other ancillary equipment. Inspect, troubleshoot, and repair auxiliary systems that support major Power Plant equipment. Perform assigned preventive and corrective maintenance activities to help ensure reliable and economical utility services. Follow established safety procedures, maintenance practices, and departmental standards. Participate in classroom training, coursework, and hands-on learning activities as part of the Utilities Department training program. Develop the technical knowledge and practical skills necessary to progress toward fully performing the responsibilities of a Mechanic B. Maintain accurate records and documentation related to maintenance activities, as required. Perform other related duties as assigned. \n\n Education One-year certificate or equivalent training from an accredited college, technical school, or trade program. Completion of a technical school program or apprenticeship program is preferred. Experience 1–2 years of related mechanical, maintenance, industrial, or utility experience. Skills and Competencies Basic knowledge of mechanical maintenance, repair, and troubleshooting principles. Ability to learn and work with industrial Power Plant and utility equipment. Ability to read and follow technical instructions, procedures, and safety requirements. Mechanical aptitude and a willingness to develop technical skills through hands-on training. Ability to work effectively as part of a team and follow direction from experienced mechanics and supervisors. Commitment to safe work practices and continuous learning. Training Requirement Successfully complete the Utilities Department training regimen for the Mechanic B position within 12 months of hire. \n\n Hourly Rate: $27.39 Resume required and cover letter preferred Application Deadline:  September 16, 202 The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Facilities/Maintenance/Utilities"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015013756": {
      "title": "Talk WithYour Baby Parent Educator",
      "description": "The Talk With Your Baby (TWYB) Parent Educator plays an important role in supporting parents and caregivers as they build meaningful connections with their young children and discover the powerful impact of everyday interactions. As part of a collaborative, multi-organization parent education initiative, this role will teach engaging classes and coordinate family-focused events that help parents understand early childhood development and their critical role in supporting it. TWYB uses research-based strategies and curriculum to help families understand how simple, everyday conversations with young children can have a lasting impact. Talking with babies and young children helps build vocabulary, cognitive development, and experiences that lay the groundwork for future reading success while also strengthening social and emotional development. In this role, you’ll have the opportunity to connect with families, share practical tools and resources, and turn research into strategies parents can use every day. Whether leading a class, facilitating a family event, or engaging with parents one-on-one, your work will help families feel confident in supporting their child’s growth and development. This is an opportunity to bring your energy, creativity, and passion for early childhood education to a program focused on helping children—and the families who support them—thrive. \n\n Bachelor’s degree in early childhood education, literacy, parent education, social work, or health-related field. 1 to 2 years of experience with: Working with young children and parents. Excellent interpersonal skills to work with people from a diversity of class, race, educational backgrounds, ethnicity, and native language. Good organizational and communication skills. Bilingual Spanish Preferred \n\n Salary: Up to $65,000 Applications will be accepted through September 16, 2026 RESUME & COVER LETTER ARE REQUIRED to be considered for this position. Please add in the attachments section of your application. The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame, founded in 1842 in Notre Dame, Indiana, is a premier American research university. As a Catholic institution, it uniquely blends faith with intellectual inquiry, striving for excellence in teaching, research, and serving the common good. Notre Dame offers a dynamic and rewarding environment for its diverse community of students, faculty, and staff, who are all dedicated to fostering an inclusive campus and being a force for good in the world. With world-class facilities, acclaimed academic programs, and a strong commitment to innovation and ethical leadership, Notre Dame is an ideal place to make a meaningful impact.",
      "department": "Administration/Leadership"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990015012626": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Corporate Relations",
      "description": "The Assistant Director for Corporate Relations (CR) supports the University of Notre Dame’s priority academic objectives through faculty engagement, concept and proposal support (including project management), and strategic corporate relationship cultivation. As a key member of the central development team, you will collaborate with faculty, staff, and administrators across the University to identify, develop, and execute academic fundraising initiatives. In this role, you will play a crucial supporting part in Development's interaction with the Academy and the Office of Notre Dame Research, maintaining the highest standards of professional ethics, integrity, and compliance with University policies and external regulations. Working alongside a dedicated team of CR professionals, you will translate and articulate complex faculty-led research, scholarship, and teaching into compelling opportunities for corporate support. You will directly assist in identifying, cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding local, regional, and national corporate partners. The incumbent will manage an entry- to mid-level portfolio of corporate and corporate foundation relationships. A successful candidate will proactively engage industry partners and internal stakeholders, ensuring rapid turnaround times and driving pipeline momentum. Key Responsibilities Faculty Engagement   Work directly with faculty to surface, synthesize, articulate, and prioritize academic research and projects for philanthropic solicitation. Socialize corporate RFPs across academic departments, Centers, and Institutes to curate compelling proposal concepts. Convene and collaborate with faculty and CR leadership to design projects aligned with corporate interests. Streamline the RFP lifecycle – from intake to submission – and assess strategic fit for incoming opportunities. Serve as a trusted liaison bridging corporate interests with institutional priorities; attend relevant faculty presentations and campus department events. Corporate Relations & Fundraising  Manage a defined portfolio of corporate and corporate foundation prospects to achieve an individual annual goal through entry- and mid-level gifts and grants. Drive all phases of the development lifecycle: identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship. Design and execute tailored strategies to expand the prospect pool and build long-term corporate relationships. ​​​​​​​Align faculty research and university priorities with corporate capacity and philanthropic focus areas. Research & Proposal Support  Partner with prospect research analysts to build targeted corporate cultivation and solicitation plans. Develop and maintain a robust annual submission schedule for open Requests for Proposals (RFPs), Letters of Inquiry (LOIs), and full proposals. Plan and execute institutional campus visits and executive meetings, preparing concise, insight-driven strategy briefs for faculty and University leadership, and external stakeholders. ​​​​​​​Coordinate with the Office of Notre Dame Research on grant-related due diligence, submissions, reporting, and compliance. Ideation & Portfolio Strategy  Cultivate and refine ideas that turn into high-impact corporate giving opportunities aligned with President, Provost, and Dean priorities. Organize workshops, webinars, and information sessions to enhance internal gift- and grant-seeking capabilities across campus. ​​​​​​​Partner with CR leadership to execute strategic plans for assigned corporate accounts. Essential Operations & Administration  Maintain timely and complete records – including contact reports and proposal statuses – in the Development database. Ensure rapid, consistent follow-through on correspondence, meetings, travel, and proposal tracking. Collaborate effectively across campus with Notre Dame Research, Research and Sponsored Programs Accounting, and college representatives. ​​​​​​​Continuously refine workflows to enhance transparency, standardization, and team operational efficiency. \n\n Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. 3-5 years of progressive experience in corporate relations, higher education fundraising, institutional advancement, grant writing, or business development/sales. Demonstrated track record managing project life cycles, drafting/coordinating complex proposals, and maintaining submission schedules. Experience managing entry- to mid-level donor or client portfolios with demonstrated progress toward quantitative targets or fundraising goals. Exceptional written and oral communication skills, including the ability to translate complex academic and scientific concepts into compelling narrative proposals for corporate executives. Experience working with a donor/constituent database, CRM (e.g., Salesforce, Advance), or project management software to track contact reports and pipeline metrics. Must be authorized to work in the United States without visa sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications Advanced degree. Direct experience working within a major research university setting, specifically collaborating with faculty, deans, and research administration offices (e.g., Office of Research). Specific experience navigating corporate foundation guidelines, grant submissions, due diligence, and compliance. Experience planning and executing high-level corporate convenings, executive strategy briefs, and interdisciplinary workshops. ​​​​​​​Proven ability to act as an effective advisor across diverse groups, bridging the gap between corporate industry priorities and faculty-led research initiatives. \n\n RESUME & COVER LETTER REQUIRED to be considered for this position. Please add in the attachments section of your application.  Salary: Up to $75,000, Commensurate with experience This position is located in South Bend, Indiana.  The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity.",
      "department": "Development/Fundraising"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990014999216": {
      "title": "Temporary | Retail Associate (Gipper's)",
      "description": "The University of Notre Dame Operations, Events, and Safety  (UOES) division is currently hiring for temporary/on-call Retail Associates (2nd & 3rd shift) at  Gipper's  in our Campus Dining department. The UOES Division oversees the University's event management, hospitality, and customer service functions, while also supporting student life, academic initiatives, research activities, and annual milestone celebrations. Retail Associate This position will be responsible for providing excellent customer service for our guests while ensuring a clean, fast and friendly multi unit environment.  Key Responsibilities: Assist guests and exceed expectations while working at a fast-paced dining establishment Assist with preparation of food as needed Stock and rotation of food inventories Operate order and point of sale equipment  Accurately follow the brand standards Wash dishes, and maintain a clean unit Perform all tasks relating to retail, safety and sanitation of food service facilities     \n\n Available to work afternoon or late night shifts (11AM-7PM or 4PM-12AM) Capable of managing time effectively with minimal supervision Positive, respectful, excellent communication and listening skills Previous food service experience is preferred Ability to maintain a standing position for extended periods of time (up to 6 hours at a time) Follow food safety procedures for handling, storing, and serving food products \n\n Pay Rate:  $17.50/hr The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": ""
