    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990014932074": {
      "title": "Temporary | IT Support Assistant",
      "description": "Join the University of Notre Dame Service Desk team in providing an unmatched customer experience to the areas we support for a variety of technical needs. Becoming a IT Support Assistant within the Office of Information Technology means working as part of a large team of other assistants to configure and deploy computers, monitor security compliance on computers, respond to technical issues in a timely manner, and other exciting responsibilities. We need creative problem solvers who can communicate with non-technical users and who are able to learn quickly and embrace change.  You are Curious - always wanting to learn new things, can understand complex processes, and never afraid to ask questions Mission oriented - Motivated to be responsive to end-users with technical needs and questions, proactively seek tasks to accomplish, and able to communicate with users at all levels within the University  Inspired - Driven to professionally develop, learn, and grow  A typical day could include some or all of: Scheduling and meeting with end-users who have technical needs Configuring computers with software, printers, and other specifications for users  Supporting our faculty within the classroom environment for their technical challenges \nHelping to maintain an up-to-date inventory of computer systems Documenting the intake and return of computers  Extending fundamental troubleshooting and assistance for technical questions as appropriate \nEscalating challenges to higher tiers of support when necessary Skillfully updating ServiceNow records  Participating in self-led professional development  \n\n Minimum Ability to work and learn independently Collaborate and problem-solve independently and with a team Good communication skills, written and verbal Customer service experience and a mindset to provide outstanding service Preferred Familiarity with Windows 11 Familiarity with macOS and iOS Outstanding customer service skills  Experience with office tools, including spreadsheets Good organizational and personal productivity practices  \n\n Deadline to Apply  (subject to change): Sunday, September 13th, 2026 Please Note : This position is exclusively on-campus, no remote work is available. We will not be offering relocation assistance or bonuses in hiring for this position. Your assigned location could be in locations outside of the main office. Shifts for this position  will be scheduled between the hours of 7:30 a.m. and 8:00 p.m. This position will average 16-18 hours per week. Hourly Rate : $17.50/hr   FY26 The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The  University of Notre Dame  is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990014929416": {
      "title": "Temporary | Tutor-CogSci Tutor",
      "description": "Build Relationships. Inspire Learning. Foster Growth.  Are you passionate about reducing academic opportunity gaps? Do you want to make a direct impact by mentoring and working with children? Join Tutor-CogSci Collaborative  as a Tutor . In this engaging, high-impact role, you will deliver evidence-based, structured tutoring that prioritizes strong mentor relationships and drives children’s academic growth - all while gaining valuable hands-on experience supported by a collaborative community of peers, researchers, and faculty. “My tutoring sessions are the best part of my week!” “Being able to witness the development of the kids is truly rewarding, and it’s just an amazing experience to watch these kids learn and master topics right before your eyes!” Our Vision To close educational opportunity gaps by expanding children’s access to high-quality, evidence-based literacy support aligned with the Science of Reading. Our Mission We serve children, educators, and our community through three core pillars: Expanding Access:  Delivering high-impact, evidence-based tutoring directly into local schools. Developing Future Leaders:  Providing a premier practicum and workforce development experience for college students preparing for youth-serving careers (educators, pediatricians, cognitive scientists, and advocates). Innovating Learning Design:  Advancing open science by developing, refining, and scaling effective tutoring practices. Position Overview As a Tutor-CogSci Tutor , you will work directly with children in grades K-3 in one-on-one or small-group settings, supporting structured a local school's literacy programs. You will be paired with your assigned students for the semester to build strong, consistent, and supportive relationships that accelerate learning. You do not need to work alone: you will be part of an active collaborative design community composed of tutoring specialists, instructional coaches, researchers, and university faculty. Beyond direct tutoring, you will have opportunities to contribute to curriculum development, observe peer sessions, collaborate on student progress reports, conduct research, and shape team recruitment. Note: Full training on high-quality tutorial principles, child safety, and evidence-based programs—along with required background checks—will be provided before you begin working with students.  What You’ll Bring to the Role Child-Centered Focus: A genuine interest in working with K-3 students and a commitment to helping them achieve their full academic potential. Relationship-Building: The ability to build supportive, consistent, and respectful connections with tutees, fellow tutors, and school partners. Growth Mindset: A willingness to accept instructional coaching, incorporate feedback, and reflect on your practice to improve student learning. Reliability & Adaptability: A dependable presence for your students, with the flexibility to navigate both in-person and virtual instructional settings. Key Responsibilities Direct Student Support: Deliver structured, evidence-based literacy instruction to assigned students in 1-on-1 or small group formats Relationship & Engagement: Build a positive, encouraging environment that honors student voice, builds confidence, and fosters academic growth. Collaborative Design & Training: Participate in team meetings, complete required safety/methodology onboarding, and engage in ongoing instructional coaching with university faculty and specialists. Progress Tracking & Reflection: Monitor student learning, collaborate on progress reports, and use data to adapt instruction to student needs. Program Innovation (Optional Opportunities): Contribute to curriculum refinement, peer observations, recruitment initiatives, or educational research projects aligned with Tutor-CogSci’s mission. Is This Role Right for You? This position is ideal if you: Are driven by purpose: You feel called to tackle social injustice in education and hold high expectations for all learners. Love to mentor: You enjoy sharing knowledge, building confidence, and supporting children’s personal and academic growth. Trust the science: You value educational research and seek out evidence-based solutions to learning challenges. Embrace continuous growth: You use feedback, reflection, and collaboration to refine your skills. Value connection & voice: You build authentic relationships, respect diverse perspectives, and view student voice as a key asset in learning. \n\n Experience: Prior experience teaching, tutoring, or working with youth in an educational environment is strongly preferred. Tech & Communication Skills: Strong interpersonal communication skills and comfort using basic educational technology. Location & Mobility: Ability to commute to designated school partner sites in Gary, IN. Transportation & Compensation Perks Company-Provided Travel: Personal vehicles are not required—we supply transportation to and from program sites. Carpool Driver Bonus: Tutors who choose to drive their own vehicle, transport fellow team members, and meet company driving requirements earn additional driving compensation. \n\n The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The  University of Notre Dame  is more than a workplace! We are a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Temporary"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990014926915": {
      "title": "Temporary | Tutor-CogSci Tutor ",
      "description": "Build Relationships. Inspire Learning. Foster Growth.  Are you passionate about reducing academic opportunity gaps? Do you want to make a direct impact by mentoring and working with children? Join Tutor-CogSci Collaborative  as a Tutor . In this engaging, high-impact role, you will deliver evidence-based, structured tutoring that prioritizes strong mentor relationships and drives children’s academic growth - all while gaining valuable hands-on experience supported by a collaborative community of peers, researchers, and faculty. “My tutoring sessions are the best part of my week!” “Being able to witness the development of the kids is truly rewarding, and it’s just an amazing experience to watch these kids learn and master topics right before your eyes!” Our Vision To close educational opportunity gaps by expanding children’s access to high-quality, evidence-based literacy support aligned with the Science of Reading. Our Mission We serve children, educators, and our community through three core pillars: Expanding Access:  Delivering high-impact, evidence-based tutoring directly into local schools. Developing Future Leaders:  Providing a premier practicum and workforce development experience for college students preparing for youth-serving careers (educators, pediatricians, cognitive scientists, and advocates). Innovating Learning Design:  Advancing open science by developing, refining, and scaling effective tutoring practices. Position Overview As a Tutor-CogSci Tutor , you will work directly with children in grades K-3 in one-on-one or small-group settings, supporting structured a local school's literacy programs. You will be paired with your assigned students for the semester to build strong, consistent, and supportive relationships that accelerate learning. You do not need to work alone: you will be part of an active collaborative design community composed of tutoring specialists, instructional coaches, researchers, and university faculty. Beyond direct tutoring, you will have opportunities to contribute to curriculum development, observe peer sessions, collaborate on student progress reports, conduct research, and shape team recruitment. Note: Full training on high-quality tutorial principles, child safety, and evidence-based programs—along with required background checks—will be provided before you begin working with students.  What You’ll Bring to the Role Child-Centered Focus: A genuine interest in working with K-3 students and a commitment to helping them achieve their full academic potential. Relationship-Building: The ability to build supportive, consistent, and respectful connections with tutees, fellow tutors, and school partners. Growth Mindset: A willingness to accept instructional coaching, incorporate feedback, and reflect on your practice to improve student learning. Reliability & Adaptability: A dependable presence for your students, with the flexibility to navigate both in-person and virtual instructional settings. Key Responsibilities Direct Student Support: Deliver structured, evidence-based literacy instruction to assigned students in 1-on-1 or small group formats Relationship & Engagement: Build a positive, encouraging environment that honors student voice, builds confidence, and fosters academic growth. Collaborative Design & Training: Participate in team meetings, complete required safety/methodology onboarding, and engage in ongoing instructional coaching with university faculty and specialists. Progress Tracking & Reflection: Monitor student learning, collaborate on progress reports, and use data to adapt instruction to student needs. Program Innovation (Optional Opportunities): Contribute to curriculum refinement, peer observations, recruitment initiatives, or educational research projects aligned with Tutor-CogSci’s mission. Is This Role Right for You? This position is ideal if you: Are driven by purpose: You feel called to tackle social injustice in education and hold high expectations for all learners. Love to mentor: You enjoy sharing knowledge, building confidence, and supporting children’s personal and academic growth. Trust the science: You value educational research and seek out evidence-based solutions to learning challenges. Embrace continuous growth: You use feedback, reflection, and collaboration to refine your skills. Value connection & voice: You build authentic relationships, respect diverse perspectives, and view student voice as a key asset in learning. \n\n Experience: Prior experience teaching, tutoring, or working with youth in an educational environment is strongly preferred. Tech & Communication Skills: Strong interpersonal communication skills and comfort using basic educational technology. Location & Mobility: Ability to commute to designated school partner sites in Gary, IN. Transportation & Compensation Perks Company-Provided Travel: Personal vehicles are not required-we supply transportation to and from program sites. Carpool Driver Bonus: Tutors who choose to drive their own vehicle, transport fellow team members, and meet company driving requirements earn additional driving compensation.   \n\n The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The  University of Notre Dame  is more than a workplace! We are a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Temporary"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990014926416": {
      "title": "Sous Chef | Shields Athletic Dining Facility",
      "description": "The Sous Chef plays a critical leadership role in the opening and daily operations of the Shields Family Dining Hall , a new facility dedicated to serving student-athletes at the University of Notre Dame. This position is responsible for supporting the Executive Chef in all culinary and operational aspects of the dining hall, ensuring excellence across financial performance, human resources, food quality, safety, and guest experience. As a member of the Notre Dame Dining Leadership Team, the Sous Chef contributes to delivering a “Best in Class” dining program aligned with the mission and goals of Notre Dame Campus Dining. In the absence of the Executive Chef, the Sous Chef assumes full responsibility for kitchen operations and leadership. Key Responsibilities Culinary & Kitchen Operations Oversee daily kitchen operations, including food ordering, preparation, production, and inventory control Ensure high standards of food quality, presentation, service, and hospitality Maintain consistency across all culinary offerings and service periods Leadership & Staff Development Supervise, train, and mentor kitchen staff to ensure optimal performance and professional growth Support recruitment, onboarding, and ongoing training initiatives Foster a positive, team-oriented work environment focused on excellence Financial & Administrative Management Assist in managing food costs, labor costs, and overall budget performance Support scheduling, purchasing, and inventory management processes Help ensure all operational metrics and financial targets are met Compliance & Safety Promote and enforce food safety, sanitation, and workplace safety standards Ensure compliance with all regulatory and university policies Facilities & Equipment Oversight Assist with maintenance and proper use of kitchen equipment and physical plant Identify and address operational or facility-related issues proactively Program Support & Collaboration Partner with Dining Hall leadership on marketing, programming, and special initiatives Contribute to apprenticeship and staff development programs Collaborate with cross-functional teams to enhance the overall dining experience Executive Chef Support Act as second-in-command in the kitchen, providing leadership and operational continuity Assume full responsibility for kitchen operations in the absence of the Executive Chef Communicate effectively with leadership regarding operations, staffing, and performance \n\n Proven experience in a high-volume culinary environment, preferably in institutional or campus dining Strong leadership and team management skills with the ability to motivate and develop staff Solid knowledge of food safety, sanitation, and regulatory compliance Experience with inventory control, purchasing, and cost management Excellent organizational, communication, and problem-solving abilities Ability to work in a fast-paced, dynamic environment while maintaining professionalism Culinary degree or equivalent professional experience preferred Flexibility to work varied schedules, including evenings, weekends, and peak service times \n\n Annual salary starting at:  $55,000 Commensurate with Experience As part of our commitment to attracting top talent, eligible new hires may receive a relocation incentive equal to a one time 10% of their annual base salary. This incentive is intended to help offset relocation and transition expenses associated with accepting the position. Eligibility requirements and payment details will be discussed during the hiring process. Application Deadline: September 13, 2026 The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
      "department": "Hospitality/Dining/Catering"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990014926306": {
      "title": "Program Manager, Poverty Initiative",
      "description": "The Notre Dame Poverty Initiative is seeking a Program Manager to support the Initiative's operational, administrative, communications, and event programming functions. Successful applicants must be self-starters, work well with faculty and staff across the University, and be passionate about Notre Dame’s mission to be a force for good in the world. The Program Manager will be supervised by the Assistant Director but will work closely with all members of the team, including but not limited to the Director, the Managing Director, and the Poverty Research Fellows Program Director. The Program Manager will play a pivotal role in communications and branding, internal and external event coordination, and student formation program support.   This is a three-year, limited-term position. Contingent on funding, the position may be extended. As part of the University's Strategic Framework, Notre Dame launched the Poverty Initiative with the goal of becoming a leader where students come to study poverty, prominent faculty come to conduct poverty-related research, and policymakers, philanthropists, and providers work to understand its causes, consequences, and permanent solutions. The work of the Poverty Initiative centers on promoting and cultivating poverty-focused academic research, moving research to impact, and educating the next generation of students dedicated to eliminating poverty.  Essential duties and responsibilities will include: Communications and Branding: Develop high quality presentations, talking points, and other materials for leadership. Translate complex research into digestible content to promote Initiative supported work. Create and design content to support monthly newsletter, maintain and enhance website, branding materials, annual reporting and other communications efforts. Poverty Research Fellows (PRF): Support the PRF Program team with tasks related to the execution of the Poverty Initiative’s signature student formation program. This support will include website maintenance, assisting with event planning and logistics, coordinating operations related to student immersive trips, and a myriad of other administrative duties related to Poverty Research Fellows activities. Administration: Support tasks associated with the successful operations of the Poverty Initiative including office management and administrative functions, such as meeting scheduling and organization, ordering supplies and equipment, coordinating weekly and quarterly team meetings, and execution of other administrative projects or tasks as assigned. Manage distribution lists and team calendars and provide support to the Finance Administration Specialist with basic bookkeeping. Event Management: Support the successful execution of events, such as working group meetings, benefactor gatherings, and other large and small convenings hosted by the Poverty Initiative partners or its partners. Support will include program design, scheduling, room booking, promotion, catering, day-of support, and other logistics.  \n\n The ideal candidate will hold a Bachelor’s degree from an accredited 4-year institution and have a minimum of two years of work experience.  Education and other relevant experience may be considered as a substitute for work experience. Skills and attributes must include: Strong commitment to the mission of Notre Dame and its efforts to be a force for good in the world. High level of proficiency in Google Workspace; ability to navigate and learn a variety of applications.  Ability to handle multiple priorities and meet deadlines while maintaining accuracy and consistency.  Interest in working with a mature team focused on a culture of collaboration, trust, and excellence. Ability to thrive in a small and dynamic team while effectively navigating a large university environment.  Strong written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently. Ability to represent the Poverty Initiative with a variety of stakeholders, from undergraduate students to high-level benefactors. \n\n RESUME & COVER LETTER ARE REQUIRED to be considered for this position. Please add in the attachments section of your application.  Starting salary is $60,000, commensurate with experience Please submit your application by September 13, 2026.    This position is an on-site position with the possibility of hybrid work location.  Benefits page: https://hr.nd.edu/benefits-compensation/ The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n For over 180 years, the University of Notre Dame has been a leading American research university, offering a distinct perspective in higher education. This Catholic institution, based in Notre Dame, Indiana, is driven by a powerful blend of faith and intellectual curiosity, consistently pursuing excellence in education, groundbreaking research, and service to society. Notre Dame fosters a welcoming and vibrant campus where students, faculty, and staff are united in their commitment to creating an inclusive community and making a difference in the world. For individuals passionate about innovation, ethical leadership, and leveraging world-class facilities and renowned academic programs, Notre Dame provides a dynamic and impactful career path.",
      "department": "Office/Clerical"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990014920901": {
      "title": "FinOps Analyst",
      "description": "At the University of Notre Dame, technology serves as a vital engine driving academic and research excellence. As our digital landscape expands across multi-cloud infrastructure and artificial intelligence, thoughtful financial stewardship is essential to ensure our technology investments sustainably support the University's mission. To lead this effort, the IT Financial Operations team within the Office of Information Technology (OIT) is establishing a dedicated Cloud FinOps function focused on usage-based consumption modeling and forecasting. We are seeking a FinOps Analyst to serve as a strategic bridge between financial analysis and technical innovation. In this role, you will partner directly with technical teams such as Cloud Storage and AI Enablement to provide cost visibility, forecasting, and decision support. By translating complex usage and vendor pricing data into actionable insights, you will help leaders make informed resource decisions that balance performance, innovation, and cost efficiency. What You'll Do Cloud and AI Cost Management: Develop a working knowledge of OIT's multi-cloud environment, artificial intelligence platforms, and pricing structures. Analyze cost and usage data to identify cost drivers, billing discrepancies, and usage patterns. Partner with technical teams to estimate future demand, improve resource utilization, and drive chargeback models. Financial Planning and Analysis: Collaborate with service owners to support usage-based forecasting models for cloud and artificial intelligence platforms. Review vendor billing and usage data to validate charges, analyze expenses against budget, and support cost scenario modeling. Strategic Partnership and Actionable Insights: Support reporting and configuration of usage-based consumption tools in ServiceNow. Monitor vendor pricing structures and metrics while translating technical and financial data into recommendations regarding tradeoffs among cost, performance, and service levels. Contribute to governance processes that support innovation and responsible consumption. \n\n Minimum Bachelor's degree. At least three years of professional experience. Ability to work in the United States, now or in the future, without visa sponsorship. Familiarity with financial modeling, data analysis, forecasting, and cost reporting. Ability to build trust and collaborate effectively with technical teams, service owners, and leadership. Strong communication skills to convey technical and financial concepts clearly. Solution-oriented mindset focused on practical, data-backed recommendations. Aptitude to develop technical fluency regarding cloud environments, artificial intelligence platforms, and pricing models. Preferred Experience supporting financial operations in higher education or complex enterprise technology environments. Advanced proficiency in financial analysis and forecasting tools. Experience and/or interest in developing expertise in Cloud FinOps, AI cost management, technology business management, or IT Financial Strategy. \n\n Please include a cover letter for full consideration of your application Deadline to apply (subject to change) : Sunday, September 13th, 2026 This is a full time position, M-F 8-5. This role may begin as a fully onsite position during an acclimation and ramp-up period. After this period, the position is eligible for a hybrid work arrangement (1 day remote) based on operational needs and team expectations. The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame, founded in 1842 in Notre Dame, Indiana, is a premier American research university. As a Catholic institution, it uniquely blends faith with intellectual inquiry, striving for excellence in teaching, research, and serving the common good. Notre Dame offers a dynamic and rewarding environment for its diverse community of students, faculty, and staff, who are all dedicated to fostering an inclusive campus and being a force for good in the world. With world-class facilities, acclaimed academic programs, and a strong commitment to innovation and ethical leadership, Notre Dame is an ideal place to make a meaningful impact.",
      "department": "Information Technology"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/UniversityOfNotreDame/3743990014918006": {
      "title": "Temporary | Event Captain",
      "description": "The  University of Notre Dame Operations, Events, and Safety  (UOES) division is currently hiring for temporary/on-call  Event Captains  in our campus Events department. The UOES Division oversees the University's event management, hospitality, and customer service functions, while also supporting student life, academic initiatives, research activities, and annual milestone celebrations. Key Responsibilities: Oversee the set up, execution, and tear down of events taking place within the stadium and any additional event locations on campus including, but not limited to; Club Naimoli, Compton Family Ice Arena, Joyce Center, etc Ensure room set up is done correctly prior to vendors arriving for event set up and assist facilities in pre event set up if needed  Touch base with the client prior to the event start time to ensure all customer expectations are met Ensure all vendors arrive at their expected time and confirm that set up has been completed, according to the agreed upon timeline Troubleshoot A/V during an event (when needed) and provide assistance with any repairs needed Collaborate with event planners and relevant University departments (e.g., Facilities, Security, Catering) to ensure seamless coordination and support Deliver the highest level of customer service for event clients, campus partners and university vendors to maintain a positive and professional working relationship Spend some time in the office prior to events to review upcoming assigned events, and work through any questions directly with the Event Specialist who planned the event Thoroughly and thoughtfully complete the post event captain’s report and the post event kitchen cleaning checklist Whether you’re gaining your first job experience or looking to grow into a leadership role, the Event Captain role is a great place to start. Apply today and become part of a fun and supportive team! \n\n Reliable, responsible, and able to work independently Ability to problem solve and rectify a situation quickly Positive attitude and respectful demeanor Strong communication and time-management skills \n\n Pay Rate: $17.50/hr The University of Notre Dame seeks to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty, staff and administration. The University is an Equal Opportunity Employer, and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, sex, disability, veteran status, genetic information, or age in employment. Moreover, Notre Dame prohibits discrimination against veterans or disabled qualified individuals, and complies with 41 CFR 60-741.5(a) and 41 CFR 60-300.5(a). We strongly encourage applications from candidates attracted to a university with a Catholic identity. \n\n The University of Notre Dame is more than a workplace; it is a vibrant, mission-driven community where every employee is valued and supported. Rooted in a tradition of excellence and inspired by our Catholic character, Notre Dame is committed to fostering an environment of care that nurtures the whole person—mind, body, and spirit. Here, you will find a deep sense of belonging, a culture that prioritizes well-being, and the opportunity to grow your career while being a force for good in the world. Whether contributing to world-class research, shaping the student experience, or supporting the University’s mission in other ways, you will be part of a dedicated team working to make a meaningful impact on campus and beyond. At Notre Dame, your work matters, and so do you!",
